course: dictionary
lesson: 13209
title: Furloughed: sent home, not sacked
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «The shop had no customers, so the workers were furloughed for a month.» Furloughed means sent home from work for a time. The workers are not at work, but they still belong to the shop.
explain: Furloughed is a gentle word. It sounds kinder than «laid off», which means the job has ended. Grown-ups often use it in the news when money is short and many workers must stay at home.
explain: Look for clues in the rest of the sentence. «Dad was furloughed, so he stayed at home and was not paid.» Not being paid is the clue: a furloughed worker may get no money, or less money, while they are away.
fact: Furloughed means sent home from work for a time, often without pay.
fact: «Furloughed» is often used when a business has no work or money.
fact: A furloughed worker may lose their job later, so the word does not always mean good news.
fact: Long ago, in the army, «furlough» meant a short leave of absence, as in «a soldier on furlough».
fact: The word is common in news about money problems in the UK and the US.
exercise: «The shop had no customers, so the workers were furloughed.» What does furloughed mean? | *Sent home from work for a time | Given a big pay rise | Asked to work extra hours | Moved to a new town
exercise: Why might a worker be furloughed? | It is their birthday | They have learned to swim | They won a prize | *Their business has no work or money
exercise: Which word sounds harsher than furloughed? | *Laid off | Smiled | Paid | Rested
test:
«Dad was furloughed, so he stayed at home and was not paid.» What clue shows that furloughed means away from work without pay? | The dog barked all night | *He was not paid | The sun was out | Mum made a cake
In the army long ago, a «furlough» meant what? | *A short leave of absence | A kind of boat | A type of hat | A hard test
exam: Jade says, «My mum was furloughed, so she is worried about money.» Why is Jade's mum worried? | She is on holiday in Spain | She has a new car | She won a race | *Her work has stopped and she may not be paid
